<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    `updatePeriodMillis` is 30 minutes because that is Android's floor — anything
    smaller is silently rounded up, so asking for less only misleads whoever
    reads this file. The app pushes fresh values whenever it is opened or a
    reminder fires, which is what actually keeps the widget current; this period
    is the backstop for a phone nobody has touched.

    ## Why `keyguard` is in the category list

    Android's lock screen has had three different answers to third-party widgets
    and this file has to satisfy all of them at once:

    * **API 17–20** offered them through `android:widgetCategory="keyguard"`
      plus `initialKeyguardLayout`. Removed in Lollipop.
    * **API 21–35** had none at all. Nothing declared here changes that.
    * **Android 16 QPR1 and later** brought them back, and made them
      **opt-out**: a widget is eligible unless it declares `not_keyguard`. The
      panel is rolling out by device — Pixel Tablet first — so a phone on
      Android 16 may still not offer it.

    Declaring `home_screen|keyguard` therefore costs nothing and covers every
    one of those: the home-screen flag is untouched, the modern platform ignores
    the keyguard flag because it does not need it, and an implementation that
    filters on the flag — an OEM lock screen, or the legacy API — finds it.

    ## Why `0x800` is there as well

    Samsung's One UI (7 and 8) *does* have a lock-screen widget panel, and it
    does not key off `keyguard` at all. Decoding the provider XML of the widgets
    that appear in it (`com.sec.android.app.clockpackage`'s TimerWidgetProvider,
    Calendar, S Health, SmartThings, Weather) shows every one of them declaring
    `android:widgetCategory=2048` — a flag with no name in the platform
    attribute, which is why the value is assembled in
    `res/values/widget_category.xml` and referenced here: aapt rejects a raw
    number in a flags attribute but accepts a reference. `0x2000` (8192) is the
    same manufacturer's category for the cover screen and is not claimed.

    Declaring it is the only way to be *eligible* there; whether One UI's picker
    then lists a third-party app is the manufacturer's decision and was not
    documented anywhere we could find. It costs nothing on any other device.

    What none of this can do is conjure the feature onto a phone whose system
    has no lock-screen widget panel, which is most phones. The app's own help
    screen says so rather than leaving the reader hunting for a list that is
    not there.
-->
<appwidget-provider xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:minWidth="180dp"
    android:minHeight="80dp"
    android:targetCellWidth="4"
    android:targetCellHeight="2"
    android:updatePeriodMillis="1800000"
    android:initialLayout="@layout/athkar_widget"
    android:initialKeyguardLayout="@layout/athkar_widget"
    android:previewLayout="@layout/athkar_widget"
    android:resizeMode="horizontal|vertical"
    android:widgetCategory="@integer/athkar_widget_category" />
